  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nAlgorithm will optimize a function represented by its autocorrelation function directly, using a penalty-based method to enforce the constraints. The key rationale is:\n- Non-negativity, boundedness and integral constraint can be formulated as exact constraints through Lagrange multipliers\n- The objective is to minimize the maximum overlap at any shift, achieved by crafting a function with minimal autocorrelation\n- The approach uses a gradient-based optimization with exponential smoothing to ensure numerical stability\n- The strategy guarantees progress toward the optimum by using a Barzilai-Borwein method with adaptive step sizes\n- Differentiability is maintained through the use of auxiliary variables to represent the function and its integral\n\nThe implementation addresses the bottleneck of previous methods by ensuring:\n- Better handling of complex constraints \n- Improved numerical stability \n- More aggressive exploration of the solution space\n- Faster convergence through smart step selection\n\nThis approach will likely yield better results than iterative refinement because it:\n- directly targets the objective\n- maintains feasible points throughout optimization\n- has less chance of converging to local optima\n- handles higher-resolution representations\n- combines multiple advanced optimization techniques\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n\nVariables: \n- Function h(x) on [0,2], let F(k) = \u222bh(x)h(x+k)dx (note: the problem has h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx)\n- However, we'll optimize L(k) = h(x) - h(x+k) directly to minimize the overlap\n\nRepresentation:\n- Use N grid points with step dx=2/N\n- Allow for interpolation when resizing\n\nObjective to minimize:\n- C5 = max_k (overlap part) = max_k (\u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx)\nEquivalent to: \n  minimize max_k (exponentially penalized overlap)\n  while satisfying:\n    \u222bh(x)dx = 1\n    0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 \u2200x\n\nConstraints:\n  - Let I = \u222bh(x)dx - 1 = 0\n  - Let L(x) = h(x) - 0 = 0 (upper bound)\n  - Let U(x) = 1 - h(x) = 0 (lower bound)\n\nOptimization formulation:\nLet F = vector of function values (N points)\nConstraints:\n  S(F) = sum(F*dx) - 1 = 0\n  0 \u2264 F_i \u2264 1 for all i\n\nObjective:\n  minimize max_k (overlap(F,k))\n\nImplementation structure:\n- Define a feasible point selector (projected gradient)\n- Use exponential barrier for constraints\n- Apply Barzilai-Borwein method\n- Include adaptive step control\n- Maintain N as a parameter with resizing option\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n\n1.1. Initialization:\n    N0 = 320 (starting resolution)\n    F0 = initial_h_values (clipped and normalized to sum to 0.5*N0)\n    Set up gradient functions for autocorrelation and constraints\n\n1.2. Gradient computation:\n    For overlap (autocorrelation penalty):\n        C(x) = \u222bh(x)h(x+k)dx for each k\n        Use trapezoidal rule calculation\n        Objective: minimize max_k C(k)\n\n    Gradient for constraint:\n        dC/dF_i = penalty * (max_k C(k) - C(k))'s distribution + constraint normals\n        Use adjoint method to distribute penalty\n\n1.3. Optimization phase:\n    Use Barzilai-Borwein method:\n        while time < budget_s:\n            if max(gradient norm) < 1e-7: break\n            s = -gradient\n            y = gradient^{k+1} - gradient^k\n            alpha = (s\u00b7y) / (y\u00b7y)  (adaptive step size)\n            s = s * alpha\n            F_new = Projection(F_old + s, feasibility=True)\n            Update step-by-step\n\n            If no progress in 10 steps:\n                Try restart by changing grid resolution\n                Try random restart\n\n1.4. Constraint handling:\n    - Clip and normalize function values to [0,1] with integral 1 using:\n        F_clipped = componentwise clip to [0,1]\n        Adjust integral by:\n            center = (sum(F_clipped * (x_range)) / sum(x_range)\n            Multiply by (sum(x_range)) / (integral adjustment)\n\n1.5. Step refinement:\n    - After phase, use small perturbations in random parameter space\n    - For very high resolution:\n        - Use exponential scaling to balance precision and local minima avoidance\n\n4. Search choices:\n\nStarting points:\n    - Base resolution: 256 grid points (dx=0.0078)\n    - Initial grid can be doubled as needed\n    - Optimization targets: \n        alpha = 5000 to \u216000000 (initial heavy penalty)\n        then decreased by factor of 1.25 each restart\n\nAdditional optimizations:\n    - Allow optional Fourier smoothing (1st Fourier component)\n    - Local tuning grid: 512 points for detail optimization\n    - Projection: ensure integral constraint is met at each step\n\nG parameter ranges:\n    - alpha from 1000 to \u2160000000\n    - Penalty decay factor: 0.9 to 0.9995\n    - Barzilai-Borwein beta = 2\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n\n    Step size: dx=2/N\n    Total evaluations: roughly 3000 per resolution change\n    Runs in grid coordinates: 2*N-1 for autocorrelation\n\n    Estimation:\n        Barzilai-Borwein: O( (budget_s/step_per_iteration) * (2*N-1) )\n        Steps per iteration: 0.5 to 1.0 seconds\n        Bad grid resolution effects: use exponential smoothing to stabilize initial phase\n\n    Check for convergence after 1000 steps with:\n        - Adjacent penalty reductions: if gradient norm is still falling by factor 0.99\n        - Accepting a smaller grid for speed or better number of points\n\n    Fallback procedure:\n        If solver fails after 2 restarts:\n            Use thresholded representation of initial solution\n            Use exponential clustering around best found points\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n\n    Return a function h_values of length n_points meeting:\n        - 0 \u2264 h_values[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n        - sum(h_values)*dx \u2248 0.995 to 1.0 (with floating-point error < 1e-3)\n\n    Final objective: \n        Compute exactly using: \n            overlap = np.max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full'))\n            c5 = overlap * dx\n\n    Return format: (h_values, c5, n_points)\n\n    Additional requirements for validity:\n        - Grid must be integer multiples of base resolution (256)\n        - Optimization must maintain constraints at each iteration\n\n    Fallback mechanism:\n        If optimization fails to converge:\n            Use resampling technique from original function\n            Use 500 small steps of Barzilai-Borwein method",
